## VERDICT: BRANCH X. NO ACT TAKEN.

Four of five read TRUE; W3 reads FALSE on its child half. Both halves name this exact reading as a
branch-X trigger in their own words -- COO half: "Branch X (anything else): any of W1-W5 reads FALSE
(A CHILD, a live connection, CPU accrued, a recent write, a changed creation time): NO act, the stop
file stays, the seat files one row naming the reading and waits."

So: no Stop-ScheduledTask, no census, no C-g delete, no Start-ScheduledTask. The stop file STAYS.
The scheduled task is UNTOUCHED and still armed at the old pin. Nothing served moved.

This seat did NOT read W3's intent as "no WORKER child" and let conhost.exe through. A console host is
plausibly not the kind of child the condition is hunting, and saying so is this seat's business; acting
on it is not. Reading a signed condition narrower than its words is the widening the pair exists to
prevent, and the halves named "a child" without qualification. The issuer decides whether W3 means to
exclude a console host.

## OBSERVATION THE ISSUER SHOULD HAVE (not a diagnosis acted on, and not a cause claimed proven)

MainWindowTitle reads, in BOTH samples:  'Select C:\Program Files\Python314\python.exe'

On Windows a console window whose title carries the 'Select' prefix is in MARK / QUICKEDIT SELECTION
mode, and a process that writes to that console BLOCKS until the selection is released. That shape
fits every reading here and explains the ones a plain hang does not:
  - zero CPU accrued and a single thread parked in a kernel Executive wait;
  - it stopped AT a job boundary -- the last event is run_record_persisted, a COMPLETED job, and the
    next thing the runner does is write a log line;
  - the conhost.exe child that makes W3 false is the console host that owns that selection;
  - Responding reads True throughout, which a deadlocked or crashed process would not sustain.
If that is what this is, the runner is NOT broken and needs NO kill: releasing the selection (a key
press in the window, or clearing QuickEdit) returns it to its loop, and it would then read the stop
file that has been waiting 68 minutes and stop cleanly, which is the ORIGINAL signed C-f path.
It is offered as the cheapest thing to try before any termination is authorised, and it is a
hypothesis consistent with the readings, not a proven cause. It belongs to row AMGT_HARVST-Z-S114,
which the issuer assigned the wedge's diagnosis.
